The gap was worse than this card assumed. On the SDK engine files are diffed out of the working directory AFTER query() returns, so every file event arrived in one burst at the end and the chat sat silent for the whole build. Separately the Editor was discarding "status" events entirely — the "Build didn't compile, fixing it" message had never been visible to anyone.

Spot-checking the mapper against 17 inputs caught three bugs: "CTASection.tsx" produced "the cta section section" (stutter plus a lowercased initialism); the API engine's project-relative paths collapsed to bare filenames; and "FAQ" was lowercased to "faq".

As this card required: every line is driven by an actual tool call. There is no timed script.` + AWAITING],

  ["Low-credit warning at the credit bar", COL.inProgress,
`Built. A real meter on the dashboard (amber before the dead end, not at it) and a tone-aware inline warning in the editor header, both showing "about N more builds" derived from that user's OWN measured average rather than a constant.

Note the sizing has moved: a real measured build came in at 472 credits, so the 750 signup grant is under two builds. estimateCredits was widened to 120-550 and the fallback raised to 400, skewed high on purpose — over-promising remaining builds is how someone hits zero mid-build.` + AWAITING],

  ["User spend caps + budget alerts", COL.inProgress,
`Spend cap built. User.spendCapCredits, enforced in app/api/generate/route.ts BEFORE anything is spent, with a /settings page to set or clear it. Rolling 30 days rather than calendar month so there is no reset day to game and no cliff mid-month.

The error message distinguishes it from running out of credits — they still HAVE the balance, they asked us not to burn past this — so it never reads as a surprise limit we imposed.

Budget ALERTS (the email half of this card) are being built separately.` + AWAITING],

  ["Activation funnel dashboard", COL.inProgress,
`Built. lib/events.ts + an Event table, with the funnel rendering on /admin: signup → first project → first successful build → first publish, counted in DISTINCT USERS rather than events (counting events would let one enthusiastic user look like retention).

North Star is set to first PUBLISH, not signup or first generation — the moment the product delivered what it promises.

Every event name in the vocabulary is verified to actually fire. Two acquisition names (landing.viewed, landing.prompt_submitted) were REMOVED because nothing emitted them: the privacy policy is written from this list, so an aspirational name makes the policy describe collection that does not happen.` + AWAITING],

  ["Build success-rate & eval dashboard", COL.inProgress,
`Half of this is built.

Added Build.repairAttempts — the green-build-first-try metric this roadmap treats as a gate had literally nothing measuring it. It now records per build, renders as a rate on /admin, and shows as a per-build column.

The eval half is scripts/eval (see the golden-set card). Not yet joined to this dashboard.

Data caveat worth knowing: 8 of 10 local builds have failed, almost all on auth, so the current green-build rate is computed off two successes. Also fixed here — failed builds used to record model:"" because the model was only written on success, making it impossible to attribute a failure spike to a model change.` + AWAITING],

  ["Project dashboard: list, rename, duplicate", COL.inProgress,
`Built: rename, duplicate and delete, with a per-row menu on the dashboard.

The slug NEVER changes on rename, and that was a deliberate call: the slug is the published address, so re-slugging would 404 every inbound link AND release a globally-unique URL for someone else to claim — a URL takeover triggered by an action that looks purely cosmetic. The UI states this inline.

Delete confirmation is graded by stakes: a published site requires typing its exact name; a draft gets an explicit two-step. Requiring name-typing on every throwaway draft just trains people to type it without reading.

Cascade behaviour was verified against the live Postgres, not assumed: files/messages/builds cascade, CreditLedger rows SURVIVE with buildId nulled — deleting the artifact does not un-charge the work, which is correct for an auditable ledger.` + AWAITING],

  ["Auto-enhance: expand a rough prompt", COL.inProgress,
`Built. app/api/enhance/route.ts + lib/enhance.ts, on Haiku, with the expanded spec landing in the composer's own textarea so accept = press Build, edit = type, discard = Revert. No new UI patterns.

Not credit-charged and creates no build row: a preview of what you are about to buy cannot itself cost credits.

Governing rule: enhance must never stand between someone and their build. Hard deadlines, and every failure path collapses to one non-destructive message with the typed prompt still in the box.

Caught during the build: with a multi-line spec in the box, the existing Enter-submits handler would have fired a build on the first paragraph break the user typed.` + AWAITING],

  ["Content policy + AUP moderation", COL.inProgress,
`Built. lib/moderation.ts, running on publish.

Every HIGH (blocking) rule requires a CONJUNCTION, never a single token — explicitly not a keyword blocklist. Rules: brand-impersonation credential forms, credential exfil channels (Telegram/Discord webhook/off-origin PHP/formsubmit inside the same form), wallet seed-phrase capture, and wallet-drainer approval primitives. Medium and low findings log and allow.

Verified against 13 fixtures, each run twice — as source AND as real vite build output. That second pass mattered: the minifier strips comments, so a name-based drainer rule went silent on built output; raw 4-byte selectors were added because they survive minification.

False-positive work was the bulk of it. A real product login page, a coffee shop using formsubmit.co, and a crypto-safety explainer saying "never share your seed phrase" all pass clean.

Known limits, stated plainly in the module: string-splitting or runtime-assembled markup defeats all of it. No attempt at evasion resistance.` + AWAITING],

  ["Code view + inline editing + export", COL.inProgress,
`The EXPORT half is built. app/api/projects/[id]/export/route.ts streams a zip via a dependency-free writer over node:zlib — no npm package added.

Runtime-tested against a fixture and read back with two independent extractors. That testing caught a real bug: 0o100644 << 16 overflows into a negative signed int32 and writeUInt32LE rejected it outright, so every download would have 500'd.

Zip-slip sanitised: "my-site/../../escape.txt" flattens correctly.

DEPLOY COUPLING — read this before shipping: several LIVE blog posts currently say "there's no zip or full-project download". That is TRUE today. Deploying export makes it false, and the two head-to-head comparison pages both list "Export / download code — Not yet". Those content edits must go out WITH the deploy.` + AWAITING],

  ["Legal pack: ToS, Privacy", COL.inProgress,
`Privacy policy and terms rewritten against what the code actually does, verified claim by claim.

The old policy described a product that no longer exists. It omitted: the entire analytics stream, build ratings, per-build full file snapshots (Build.filesSnapshot), social reward connections, the spend cap, and Discord as a data recipient. It also claimed publishes are scanned ONLY for secrets, when the abuse scan does considerably more.

Retention was wrong by omission — there is no pruning job anywhere in the repo, so the honest statement is "retained until you delete the project", with events retained indefinitely.

NOT DONE and needs a human: no AUP or DPA. Two placeholders are sitting in both pages — the operating legal entity/address, and governing law/jurisdiction — because nothing in the codebase names them. privacy@kodely.me is now the stated route for deletion requests but the only mailbox in code is hello@kodely.me; confirm the alias exists or the promise is empty.

No GDPR/CCPA/SOC2 claims were made. This is written to be factually accurate about the code, not to be legally sufficient.` + AWAITING],

  ["Deleted sites can stay cached", null,
`Partially addressed. Delete now correctly takes a published site offline — verified 200 before, 404 after, with the cascade removing the published rows and the Project row the slug resolves by.

The 60s cache window in this card is still open and is still the right concern for an urgent takedown.

Separately fixed while here: pruned checkpoints used to keep rendering in the history panel and 404 on click. That query now filters on filesSnapshot not being null, and is bounded at 100 (it was unlimited). Proven with a rolled-back transaction rather than assumed — Prisma's DbNull/JsonNull distinction is a known footgun and my first test was worthless because every build in the DB had a snapshot.`],
];

const findCard = db.prepare(
  `SELECT c.id, c.title, c.columnId, co.name AS col
   FROM cards c JOIN columns co ON co.id = c.columnId
   WHERE co.boardId = ? AND c.archivedAt IS NULL AND c.title LIKE ?`,
);
const addComment = db.prepare(
  "INSERT INTO comments (id, cardId, authorName, body) VALUES (?, ?, 'Claude', ?)",
);
const maxPos = db.prepare("SELECT COALESCE(MAX(position), -1) AS p FROM cards WHERE columnId = ?");
const moveCard = db.prepare("UPDATE cards SET columnId = ?, position = ? WHERE id = ?");

let moved = 0;
let commented = 0;
for (const [needle, target, body] of UPDATES) {
  const card = findCard.get(BOARD, `%${needle}%`);
  if (!card) {
    console.log(`!! NOT FOUND: ${needle}`);
    continue;
  }
  addComment.run(randomUUID(), card.id, body);
  commented++;
  let note = "commented";
  if (target && card.columnId !== target) {
    const pos = maxPos.get(target).p + 1;
    moveCard.run(target, pos, card.id);
    moved++;
    note = `${card.col} -> In Progress`;
  }
  console.log(`${note.padEnd(28)} | ${card.title.slice(0, 62)}`);
}
console.log(`\n${commented} commented, ${moved} moved.`);

